# SwiftFormat configuration for PinCal.
#
# Every value below was measured against the 184 first-party Swift files, not
# assumed. Options that matched the existing code exactly (trailing commas,
# import grouping, indent-case, xcode/indent-strings) produce zero churn and are
# kept as explicit locks so they cannot drift.
#
# Companion linter: .swiftlint.yml disables every rule that is both
# "correctable" and "style", so the two tools never disagree about the same line.

# The app target and all 8 SPM packages are SWIFT_VERSION / swift-tools-version
# 6.0. Without this SwiftFormat disables syntax-aware formatting and warns.
--swiftversion 6.0

# Third-party SPM checkouts and machine-generated code are not ours to restyle.
# The 7 Packages/*/.build directories hold ~2100 dependency sources (ObjectBox,
# swift-collections, swift-testing, ...).
#
# The glob form is required: `excluded` paths are matched as prefixes, so a bare
# `.build` or `generated` does not reach Packages/<Name>/.build.
#
# LocalizedStringKeyExtension.swift is generated by GenerateLocalization and is
# rewritten by any build that touches the string catalog, so it never converges -
# formatting it only produces churn on the next build. AGENTS.md already flags
# this file as "do not commit".
--exclude **/generated/**,.build,Pods,Carthage,vendor,**/LocalizedStringKeyExtension.swift

# --- Indentation ---
# Measured: 18741 space-indented lines, 0 tab-indented, 0 CRLF files.
# 4 is both the dominant indent step and SwiftFormat's default.
--indent 4
--tab-width 4
--linebreaks lf
# Existing code does not re-indent continuation lines or wrapped strings, so
# leave both off rather than reflowing every call site.
--indent-strings false
--xcode-indentation false

# --- Line length: deliberately NOT enforced ---
# Measured p50 = 37, p90 = 89, p99 = 109, max = 180. The existing code already
# carries ~98 lines over 120. Forcing --max-width 120 was tested and rejected:
# it cannot shorten long log literals, it just explodes them, e.g.
#   navLog.debug("preferredCompactColumn \(...) -> \(...)")
# becomes four wrapped lines whose string argument is still >120 chars.
--max-width none

# --- Braces / control flow ---
# Existing code is genuinely split (55 `} else {` vs 71 next-line), so this is a
# choice rather than a measurement. Same-line is SwiftFormat's default and the
# dominant modern Swift convention; normalising costs ~26 lines, once.
--else-position same-line
--indent-case false
--guard-else auto
--wrap-conditions preserve
--wrap-effects preserve
--empty-braces no-space
--ifdef indent

# --- Wrapping ---
--wrap-arguments before-first
--wrap-parameters before-first
--wrap-collections preserve

# --- Ranges: tight, Swift-style ---
# The default noSpaceOperators list does NOT cover range operators, so the repo
# had a mix of `(0...14)` and `(0 ... 14)`. Both forms must be listed: with only
# `...`, `..<` is left spaced.
--no-space-operators ...,..<

# --- Imports: NOT handled here ---
#
# SwiftFormat cannot express "system modules first, then application modules".
# `--import-grouping` only accepts alpha, access-control, length, testable-first
# and testable-last, and all of them collapse to a single alphabetical block.
# Measured: given the correct grouping already in place,
#     import SwiftUI / import UIKit / import Foundation
#     <blank>
#     import DSKit / import CoreDomain
# `--import-grouping alpha` flattened it to one alphabetical run, deleting the
# separator; Apple's own `swift-format` does not reorder imports at all.
#
# So import grouping is delegated to scripts/sort_imports.py and SwiftFormat is
# kept away from the import block:
#   sortImports              would re-sort the block and lose the grouping
#   blankLinesBetweenImports would delete the blank line between the groups
#   blankLineAfterImports    reads a comment inside the block as the block's end
#                            and inserts a blank line before it, which the script
#                            then removes - the two fought over 3 test files
# All three are disabled below. The script owns ordering, grouping and the single
# blank line separating the block from the code that follows; with these off,
# SwiftFormat leaves imports byte-exact (verified: a deliberately mis-ordered
# block survives a run untouched).
--disable sortImports
--disable blankLinesBetweenImports
--disable blankLineAfterImports

# --- Types / expressions ---
--void-type void
# Redundant `self.` is removed (Swift API Design Guidelines). Touches 61 files;
# the alternative --self init-only only saves 82 lines and leaves the
# inconsistency in place. Safe inside escaping closures because SwiftFormat only
# drops `self.` where it is not required for disambiguation.
# Explicit `self.` is left exactly as the author wrote it.
#
# SwiftFormat's `--self` option only offers `insert`, `remove` and `init-only`;
# none of them means "leave it alone", so the RULE is disabled instead.
#
# `--self remove` stripped `self.` from 61 sites here and broke the build. Every
# one was inside an OSLog string interpolation, e.g.
#   navLog.debug("... -> \(String(describing: self.preferredCompactColumn))")
# OSLog message interpolation is an autoclosure context where the compiler
# *requires* explicit `self.` for capture semantics:
#   error: reference to property 'preferredCompactColumn' in closure requires
#          explicit use of 'self' to make capture semantics explicit
# (`RootNavigation.swift:43`) SwiftFormat's parser does not model that context, so
# it cannot tell the removal is illegal. `--self init-only` is not safe either: it
# still stripped `self.` from 17 sites, including the same two log calls, because
# `didSet` accessors are treated as part of the initialisation family.
#
# This is a formatter guessing at semantics. Explicit `self.` is also the prevailing
# style in this codebase already, so there is nothing to gain by risking it.
--disable redundantSelf
--trailing-commas always
--trim-whitespace always
--strip-unused-args closure-only

# Keep the author's `///` vs `//` choice verbatim. The default
# (--doc-comments before-declarations) demotes `///` to `//` on statements inside
# function bodies, which is arguably more correct for DocC but discards author
# intent across 13 lines of real explanatory comments.
--doc-comments preserve

# conditionalAssignment rewrites `if x { y = 1 } else { y = 2 }` into a ternary.
# That is a refactor, not formatting, so a formatter has no business doing it.
--disable conditionalAssignment

# preferKeyPath rewrites `{ $0.foo }` as `\.foo`. That is unsafe wherever the
# closure parameter is a throwing function type, because a key-path-as-function
# does not carry the `throws` signature: the compiler picks the throwing overload
# and then reports the call as unhandled. It broke the test build, e.g. in
# PCEventBatchAssembleUnitOfWorkTests.swift:318:
#   -  recolored.batch.events.allSatisfy { $0.colorName.isEmpty },
#   +  recolored.batch.events.allSatisfy(\.colorName.isEmpty),
#   error: call can throw, but it is not marked with 'try'
# `allSatisfy`, `contains(where:)` and friends all take throwing closures.
#
# Note the rule is `preferKeyPath`, NOT `redundantClosure`: measured against the
# pristine file, disabling `redundantClosure` still produced the key path, and
# only disabling `preferKeyPath` prevented it.
--disable preferKeyPath
